+A moved revision or a new prompt does not mean the game thread is parked: it may already hold the next queued action, such as the vote that starts a restore. So the game thread holds `gameLock` for the whole match and releases it only around the poll in `takeAction`, and `getSnapshotJson` takes it; a snapshot then waits for the next park instead of walking collections the game is changing (`ConcurrentModificationException`, which ended the game on a seat snapshot). Any new read of game state from a host thread must take `gameLock` too. Because the game cannot change while it is parked, `pollAction` opens a `SnapshotRound` before it releases the lock and drops it when it takes the lock back: every seat's snapshot during one park shares it, so each creature's `cantBlock`/`cantAttack`/`mustAttack` (and each controller's possible defenders) is computed once per park, not once per seat. A snapshot taken outside a park (tests, the browser bridge, prompts) gets a fresh round; anything cached in a round must be a pure function of game state.
 
